videantur, fusse, a Moesogothic spelling not uncommon in English manuscripts for funze, fuæ, rue, hio, zehepen credant, ðæm, biezlan, ðpncet putant, pcomað, zecieppeb, æppærð, ielberðe, honnobe, picpieað, heifcan, ponbineð tolerat, hiepbap pastores, zecniopon, cpið Christus, and so on without end. The evidence — which this is not a convenient place to discuss — is sufficient that in this system of vocalization, whether of terminations or other syllables, we have the dialect of King Alfred's court. One editor of Orosius has furnished us, at the expense of Mr. Tollemache, with facsimiles of three pages of the Lauderdale manuscript. Of the antiquity and superior value of this manuscript there can be no doubt. We there see ie for river, as well as ea, hætt for calls, æpielme for source, pinperc, ziet, ponun, pnom for pnam, hiepa and hiopa, hoppc for hopp. Just as was to be expected from current notions, the editor who had access to this good manuscript did not use it; it has, says he, "a northerly aspect." This expression might have been valid had it been used of a manuscript of the eleventh century; but the Lauderdale manuscript is older, and agrees in spelling with others of nearly the same age. If the book called Cædmon's is indeed his — which I neither assert nor deny — the copy we have is much later than his times; but it exhibits proof of having been transcribed from an earlier book in which the same method of vocalizing prevailed. The penman altered the spelling as he went, as was customary; but at page 55 of the manuscript, line 3, he came upon a phrase from the earlier pen which he was unable to understand: it had been meant for

"ponne ic popð pciol" — meaning: when I shall pass away.

On page 18 of the manuscript, line 20, the penman forgot for a moment to alter the ancient orthography, and he put zieman pær znunber;
